- **#7626 (#7617):** unnamed Go composite types now get structured shapes (slice, map, array) through a shared capability. Java output is unchanged.

Each still needs one identity commit, and I've reminded the coordinator about both pairs and the reason-label ruling.

The Java session accepted my review of its Go bridge (#7624). The reviewer flagged one thing: a "complete and empty" frontier mixed with a string frontier could make the bridge say "string". My ruling is that skipping a genuinely complete, empty frontier is logically correct. The real defect was the false emptiness, which #7625 fixes at the root, for the bridge and for the earlier value-type barriers that share the same code. So #7624 will merge #7625's final head and add a mixed-evidence test that must come out undecided. The type-parameter decline stays as agreed.

`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
- This is a recurring choice: guard at the consumer, or fix the producer. Treating every empty frontier as undecided in the shared loop would make every consumer give up precision for unreachable paths. Fixing the producer, so "complete and empty" is only published when it is true, keeps the consumer logic simple and correct for every caller at once.
- The landing order is enforced through Git history: #7624 merges #7625's head, so MergeMarshall can't land the bridge before the fix it depends on.
`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`

